nes in front of it."],"better":"19…g5 pushes the pawn storm towards the king and takes h4 and f4 away from the queen. Engine line: 20.Bxc4 Qxc4 21.Rxd6 Nxe4 22.Qe5 Nf6 (+0.08)."},{"ply":39,"name":"20.Rd4","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"20.Rd4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Adds a decisive attacker on the knight on c4: 2 attackers against 1 defender.","Shores up the e4 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (2 against 1).","The next best move, 20.Bxc4, would drop White's winning chances from 70% to 58% (+0.83).","20.Rd4 threatens Rxc4, winning the queen for a rook.","It threatens Rxc4, winning the queen for a rook, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black answers 20…b5, leaving White with no bishop pair."]},{"ply":48,"name":"24…Qb6","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"24…Qb6 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 42% to 20%.","points":["White answers 25.e5: the pawn kicks the knight on f6, which has to move.","Better was 24…Ra8 (+0.86): the rook takes the open a-file, aiming at the rook on a1."],"better":"24…Ra8 threatens …Rxa1, winning a rook for a pawn, and takes the open a-file, aiming at the rook on a1. After 25.Rxa8 Rxa8 26.Rxc4 Qb7 27.Qxh6 Bc6, the rooks are traded and Black is two pawns down (+0.86)."},{"ply":49,"name":"25.e5","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"25.e5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The pawn kicks the knight on f6, which has to move.","Protects the rook on d4, which was hanging.","The next best move, 25.Nf3, would drop White's winning chances from 80% to 64% (+1.58).","25.e5 stops …Qxd4 and threatens exf6, winning a knight.","Black was threatening …Qxd4, winning a rook. e5 takes care of it.","It threatens exf6, winning a knight, so Black has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":51,"name":"26.Rxd7","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"26.Rxd7!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on d7 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 26…Nxd7 27.Qxe7 Qd8 28.Qd6 the engine rates it +3.68.","Takes the bishop on d7.","The rook reaches the seventh rank, where 1 pawn sits and the king is cut off on the back rank.","26.Rxd7 wins a bishop for a rook and trades off the attacked rook instead of losing it to …exd4.","Takes the bishop on d7 and wins a bishop for a rook once the captures settle."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"26.Rxd7!! offers the rook on d7. Taking it gives White the material back and more."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"26.Rxd7 puts the rook on d7 where it can be taken by the knight on f6. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the bishop on d7."},{"title":"If Black takes the rook","text":"26…Nxd7 is met by 27.Qxe7, taking the bishop on e7. Then 29.Nxc4 takes the c4 pawn, and 30.Nxd6 takes the queen on d6. White ends up two pawns ahead: White is winning (+4.27). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 26.Rxc4, is clearly weaker (+0.07 against +3.68). Black answers 26…Rxc4, taking the rook on c4 and threatening …Rxh4, winning the queen. That would leave White level."}]},{"ply":56,"name":"28…Ra8","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"28…Ra8 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 15% to 4%.","points":["White answers 29.Nxg5, which takes the pawn on g5.","After Nxg5, White wins 1 point of material within 2 moves.","Better was 28…f6 (+4.77): shores up the g5 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (3 against 1)."],"better":"28…f6 stops Bxg5. After 29.Qg6+ Kh8 30.h4 Rc7 31.Qh6+ Kg8 32.hxg5 fxg5 33.Bxg5 Bxg5 34.Qxg5+ Kh7, the bishops are traded and Black is a pawn down (+4.77)."},{"ply":57,"name":"29.Rb1","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"29.Rb1 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 96% to 80%.","points":["Black answers 29…f5, which attacks the knight on e4.","Better was 29.Nxg5 (+8.89), which takes the pawn on g5."],"better":"29.Nxg5 threatens Qh7#, with mate in 1, and wins the queen for 8 points of material. After 29…Nf6 30.Nce4 Qxf2+ 31.Kxf2 Nxe4+ 32.Nxe4 Rxa1 33.Ng5 Bxg5 34.Qxg5+ Kh8, the knights are traded and White comes out a piece and a pawn up (+8.89)."},{"ply":62,"name":"31…Ra7","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"31…Ra7 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 23% to 8%.","points":["White answers 32.Qh6+: check, and Black has only one legal reply.","Better was 31…f5 (+3.31), which attacks the knight on e4."],"better":"31…f5 threatens …fxe4, winning a knight. After 32.Qh6+ Kg8 33.Nxg5 Nf6 34.Be3 Qc6, Black is a pawn down (+3.31)."}],"opening":{"name":"Sicilian Defense: Najdorf Variation","eco":"B95","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 9…Qc7: 18 moves in all, 9 by White and 9 by Black.","Theory runs out after 9…Qc7: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}